Why this exists
---------------

The App Store's terms bind the people who download an app in ways the GPL says a
recipient must not be bound — a copy tied to a limited number of devices, and
technical measures controlling how it is installed. Distributing GPLv3 software
there without this permission puts the distributor in breach, which is what got VLC
pulled from the store. Only the copyright holder can lift that, and this file is
that lifting.

It is deliberately written for any app store rather than for Apple alone: the same
conflict arises anywhere a platform's terms restrict what the GPL leaves free, and
naming one vendor would mean writing this again for the next one.

It grants no rights over the source. Anyone who receives a copy from an app store
still receives it under the GPL and can still ask for and redistribute the source.
